The screen has identified 31 high-impact hits, revealing several critical regulatory pathways for IFNG production. Key observations: (1) Negative regulators dominate the hits (ZAP70, VAV1, TRAF6, LCK, JAK1 showing strong negative scores), suggesting TCR/kinase signaling nodes are essential suppressors; (2) Positive hits (TSC1, NFKB2, RIPK1, SOCS3) indicate mTOR and NF-κB pathways promote IFNG; (3) The tested genes show smaller effect sizes, suggesting we've now moved beyond initial screening to pathway expansion. Next steps should focus on: identifying co-regulators of the high-impact hits, exploring parallel immune signaling branches (CD28 co-stimulation, cytokine signaling), and testing genes in related kinase cascades and epigenetic modifier families.

# 2. Research Plan

The strongest hits cluster in TCR signaling (ZAP70, VAV1, LCK, JAK1), NF-κB/MAPK pathways (RELB, RELA, NFKB2, CHUK), and IFN-γ receptor feedback (IFNGR1/2). Strategy: (1) Test upstream TCR co-activators and adapters (CD28 pathway, PAX5, CARD11, SLP76) to confirm signal initiation patterns; (2) Expand kinase families showing strong effects (SRC-family, MAP kinase phosphatases, JAK/STAT modulators); (3) Test epigenetic regulators related to KDM1A/KMT2A hits that may remodel IFNG loci; (4) Include negative feedback mediators (phosphatases, ubiquitin ligases) structurally similar to high-hit regulators; (5) Sample immune checkpoint and co-stimulatory molecules (CD28, CD40, ICOS) to understand signal amplification. This approach leverages pathway knowledge while avoiding redundant testing of already-screened genes.

# 3. Solution

